Identify clay phases with structural, chemical, and preparation evidence (@mp-clay: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @mineralogy-and-petrology, #source "../sources/USGS-X-ray-Powder-Diffraction-Manual.lingua#r_3S2AET1HS289HRQBDJ87A30DX6", #source "../sources/Mineralogy-Perkins.lingua#r_3Y3EJ93EG4V7Q0TZBCRRXVNVKP", #source "../sources/MIT-Sedimentary-Geology.lingua#r_7FWZZ66CT5G1FP2JPQMWRV1YHM", #mineralogy-and-petrology "Evaporites-silica-phosphates-and-organic-associated-materials.lingua#r_6HTY4SNKY5J2B4REKH2QWDXQXT") { r_1YK3X2Z2EPXKF4P85R27T8A6FF
**Read:** [A laboratory manual for X-ray powder diffraction — Poppe and colleagues](../sources/USGS-X-ray-Powder-Diffraction-Manual.lingua#r_3S2AET1HS289HRQBDJ87A30DX6), the complete manual with the full clay-treatment and identification procedures; [Mineralogy — Dexter Perkins, open editions](../sources/Mineralogy-Perkins.lingua#r_3Y3EJ93EG4V7Q0TZBCRRXVNVKP), the entire clay/sheet-mineral treatment; [Sedimentary Geology — John Southard, MIT 12.110](../sources/MIT-Sedimentary-Geology.lingua#r_7FWZZ66CT5G1FP2JPQMWRV1YHM), the complete shale/clay and diagenetic context.

Clay-sized material is a grain-size category; clay minerals are structural/chemical classes. A fine fraction can contain other minerals, amorphous material, organic matter, and mixed assemblages. Identify the separation, preparation, and target before equating a size fraction with one mineral population.

Layer type, interlayer charge/components, hydration, ordering, and mixed layering influence diffraction and behavior. Preferred orientation and chemical/thermal treatments can provide useful contrasts under a documented procedure. A changed spacing is an observation whose interpretation depends on preparation, conditions, and alternative phases.

Clay mineralogy can record source, weathering, diagenesis, hydrothermal change, or mixtures, with important consequences for swelling, sorption, permeability, and processing. Those properties require their own measurement/model; a tentative diffraction identification does not specify all behavior at every fluid condition.

**Work:** read the entire XRD manual and reconstruct a multi-treatment clay-identification example. Compare candidate single-phase and mixed-layer explanations, preserving observed patterns and method conditions. Relate one transformation to component/water exchange and identify an independent chemical or microscopic check. Explain how uncertain identification affects a proposed material-use inference.

Read the full sheet/clay descriptions and relevant diagenetic notes. Actual preparation and instrument competence requires suitable supervised laboratory work; interpretation of published patterns is a valuable but separately described achievement.

